rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))

q('Which pattern most strongly supports an upper motor neuron lesion?', 'Pyramidal weakness with velocity-dependent increased tone and pathological extensor plantar response',[
 ('Marked focal wasting and fasciculation with reduced reflexes alone','This favors LMN dysfunction.'),('Fatigable ptosis with normal reflexes','This suggests a junctional disorder.'),('Isolated burning pain with normal power','This suggests sensory involvement rather than an UMN syndrome.')],
 'Motor localization combines power, tone and reflexes', '**UMN lesions interrupt descending motor pathways.**', ['The acute phase can be flaccid before typical spasticity emerges.','A single brisk reflex is not enough.'], '**Look for a coherent distribution.**', ['Brain and spinal lesions have different associated signs.','Use sensory, cranial and sphincter findings to refine the site.'], [['Tone','Often spastic later'],['Reflexes','Often increased'],['Plantars','May be extensor']], [EX])
q('Which pattern most strongly supports lower motor neuron involvement?', 'Weakness with neurogenic wasting, reduced reflexes and fasciculations in an appropriate distribution',[
 ('Spasticity with extensor plantars as the only pattern','This favors UMN dysfunction.'),('Pure loss of word meaning','This is a cortical cognitive deficit.'),('A visual field cut alone','This localizes to visual pathways.')],
 'The final motor pathway has a recognizable injury pattern', '**Anterior horn, root, plexus and nerve lesions can all produce LMN findings.**', ['Distribution helps distinguish them.','Atrophy and denervation take time to develop.'], '**Identify the anatomical level before naming a disease.**', ['Sensory findings can separate some nerve/root disorders from motor-neuron disease.','Use electrophysiology when it answers a focused question.'], [['Sites','Horn, root, plexus or nerve'],['Reflexes','Often reduced'],['Localization','Distribution is essential']], [EX])
q('A cranial-nerve deficit on one side accompanies weakness of the opposite limbs. Which region is particularly suggested?', 'Brainstem',[
 ('One distal peripheral nerve','This cannot combine a cranial deficit and contralateral long-tract signs.'),('A pure muscle disorder','The crossed focal pattern is not explained.'),('The neuromuscular junction alone','Junctional dysfunction does not cause this crossed long-tract localization.')],
 'Crossed signs can place a lesion between nuclei and descending tracts', '**A brainstem lesion can involve an ipsilateral cranial pathway and contralateral body pathways.**', ['Exact cranial findings refine the level.','Not every brainstem lesion produces a complete textbook syndrome.'], '**Use onset to guide urgency.**', ['An acute pattern can represent stroke.','Assess airway, swallowing and eye movements.'], [['Cranial signs','Often ipsilateral'],['Long-tract body signs','Often contralateral'],['Level','Brainstem']], [EX], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Right lower facial weakness occurs with relatively preserved forehead movement and left hemispheric signs. Which mechanism best fits?', 'Supranuclear facial weakness from a left corticobulbar lesion',[
 ('A complete right facial nerve lesion as the best explanation','That commonly affects upper and lower ipsilateral face.'),('An isolated left facial nerve lesion','This would affect the left face.'),('Forehead sparing proves that no central lesion exists','It can support a supranuclear pattern.')],
 'Bilateral upper-face input explains relative sparing', '**Corticobulbar injury commonly weakens the contralateral lower face more than the forehead.**', ['The pattern is useful but not absolutely uniform.','A pontine nuclear/fascicular lesion can produce a peripheral-type pattern.'], '**Integrate other neurological signs.**', ['Acute facial weakness can require stroke assessment.','Do not classify from forehead movement alone.'], [['Supranuclear','Often lower-face predominant'],['Nerve/nucleus','Often whole ipsilateral face'],['Context','Other signs refine localization']], [CN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Acute painful ptosis, a down-and-out eye and a dilated pupil develop. What is the most important next principle?', 'Urgently assess a third-nerve palsy for compressive aneurysm or other structural causes',[
 ('Assume diabetic microvascular palsy without imaging assessment','A compressive cause needs urgent consideration.'),('Pain alone reliably distinguishes all benign from aneurysmal palsies','Pain is not sufficiently discriminating.'),('Pupil involvement is irrelevant','It increases concern for compression.')],
 'A threatened cranial nerve can signal a vascular emergency', '**Posterior communicating artery aneurysm is a classic cause of pupil-involving third-nerve palsy.**', ['Herniation and other lesions are alternatives.','Pupil sparing is not an absolute guarantee of safety in every presentation.'], '**Arrange urgent vascular/anatomical assessment.**', ['Use the local emergency neuro-ophthalmic pathway.','Do not wait for additional deficits.'], [['Third nerve','Ptosis/ophthalmoplegia'],['Pupil dilation','Compression concern'],['Action','Urgent evaluation']], [TH,PU], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Ptosis and miosis produce anisocoria that is greater in darkness. Which pathway is impaired?', 'Oculosympathetic pathway',[
 ('Parasympathetic fibers of the third nerve as the classic mechanism','Their impairment more often dilates the pupil.'),('The optic nerve alone','This produces an afferent visual deficit rather than the classic small-pupil ptosis pattern.'),('A single extraocular muscle only','This does not explain the autonomic pupil pattern.')],
 'Compare anisocoria in light and dark', '**Horner syndrome reflects sympathetic interruption.**', ['The small pupil dilates poorly in darkness.','Lesion level and cause require a focused assessment.'], '**Treat acute painful Horner syndrome as a warning.**', ['Carotid dissection is an important cause.','Other central, chest and neck causes depend on the associated signs.'], [['Light/dark','Worse anisocoria in darkness'],['Pupil','Miosis'],['Pathway','Sympathetic']], [PU], 'clinical_scenario')
q('During a swinging flashlight test, both pupils dilate relatively when light moves from the healthy eye to the affected eye. Which abnormality is suggested?', 'Relative afferent pupillary defect',[
 ('A purely efferent third-nerve deficit as the required cause','Afferent asymmetry changes the input to both pupils.'),('Normal symmetric retinal/optic nerve function','The asymmetry suggests reduced afferent input.'),('A neuromuscular-junction defect','The light reflex does not depend on skeletal endplate transmission.')],
 'Compare the input rather than only the pupil size', '**An RAPD indicates asymmetric afferent visual pathway dysfunction.**', ['Optic neuropathy and severe retinal disease are important causes.','Bilateral symmetric disease may not produce an RAPD.'], '**Assess acuity, color and fields.**', ['Do not equate an RAPD with one unique etiology.','Urgency depends on the clinical onset and associated findings.'], [['Afferent input','Asymmetric'],['Responses','Both pupils change'],['Cause','Optic nerve or severe retinal disease']], [PU])
q('Temporal visual fields are lost in both eyes. Which localization is most characteristic?', 'Optic chiasm',[
 ('Right optic nerve alone','That causes monocular rather than bitemporal field loss.'),('Left occipital cortex alone','That usually produces a right homonymous defect.'),('Bilateral facial nerves','These do not carry visual field information.')],
 'Crossing nasal retinal fibers carry temporal visual fields', '**Chiasmal compression can produce bitemporal hemianopia.**', ['Pituitary-region lesions are an important cause.','Patterns can be incomplete or asymmetric.'], '**Assess endocrine and pressure-related symptoms.**', ['Formal perimetry and appropriate imaging refine the problem.','Acute severe headache or visual decline can require emergency assessment.'], [['Nasal retinal fibers','Cross in chiasm'],['Visual deficit','Temporal fields'],['Common region','Sellar/suprasellar']], [EX])
q('The right visual hemifield is absent in both eyes. Which side of the retrochiasmal pathway is involved?', 'Left',[
 ('Right optic nerve alone','This would affect only the right eye.'),('Right retrochiasmal pathway','This typically affects the left hemifield.'),('The optic chiasm as the uniquely required site','The homonymous pattern localizes behind the chiasm.')],
 'A homonymous defect localizes posterior to the chiasm', '**The contralateral hemifield is represented in each retrochiasmal pathway.**', ['Optic tract, radiation and occipital lesions can cause the pattern.','Congruity and associated signs help refine location.'], '**Consider onset and functional safety.**', ['An acute defect can be a stroke.','Assess reading, navigation and driving implications.'], [['Deficit','Same hemifield in both eyes'],['Side','Contralateral pathway'],['Sites','Tract, radiation or cortex']], [EX])
q('How is aphasia distinguished from isolated dysarthria?', 'Aphasia impairs language processing, while dysarthria primarily impairs motor speech production',[
 ('Any unclear speech proves aphasia','Articulation can be impaired with language intact.'),('Dysarthria necessarily impairs reading comprehension','It does not by definition.'),('Aphasia affects only voice volume','Language deficits extend beyond loudness.')],
 'Test the function rather than the sound alone', '**Naming, comprehension, repetition, reading and writing characterize language.**', ['Dysarthria can occur with intact language formulation.','Apraxia of speech is another distinct motor-planning problem.'], '**Account for hearing, language background and consciousness.**', ['Use appropriate communication support.','Acute new speech/language deficits require urgent evaluation.'], [['Aphasia','Language'],['Dysarthria','Motor execution'],['Assessment','Several language domains']], [MT])
q('A patient ignores the left side of a plate and fails to attend to left-sided stimuli despite sufficient primary sensation. Which process is most likely?', 'Left hemispatial neglect, often from right hemispheric attention-network injury',[
 ('Pure left optic nerve disease as a complete explanation','Neglect is an attentional syndrome rather than only monocular visual loss.'),('A primary muscle disorder','This cannot account for spatial inattention.'),('The patient must be deliberately refusing to cooperate','Neglect can impair awareness of the deficit.')],
 'Attention and primary sensory detection are different', '**Neglect can affect visual, tactile and representational space.**', ['Cancellation, drawing and double simultaneous stimulation can help assessment.','Visual field loss can coexist.'], '**Assess real-world consequences.**', ['Falls, eating and self-care can be affected.','Rehabilitation needs differ from correction of a primary visual deficit.'], [['Primary sensation','May be adequate'],['Attention','Spatial bias'],['Function','Major safety impact']], [MT], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient cannot demonstrate how to use a key on command despite understanding the request and having adequate strength and sensation. Which deficit is suggested?', 'Apraxia',[
 ('Paresis as the complete explanation','The required power is present.'),('Primary sensory loss alone','Sensation is adequate in the scenario.'),('Dysarthria','This concerns speech execution rather than learned skilled action.')],
 'Learned action requires more than force', '**Apraxia is impaired performance of learned purposeful actions beyond elementary motor or sensory deficits.**', ['Dominant parietal and connected networks are important.','Test several actions and imitation as appropriate.'], '**Exclude comprehension and task-familiarity problems.**', ['A single failed unfamiliar gesture is not sufficient.','Relate findings to daily function.'], [['Power','Adequate'],['Comprehension','Adequate'],['Deficit','Learned action planning/performance']], [MT], 'clinical_scenario')
q('An object is seen clearly but cannot be recognized visually; the patient recognizes it by touch. Which deficit is suggested?', 'Visual agnosia',[
 ('Complete blindness','The object is perceived and visual recognition rather than input is impaired.'),('Pure anomia as the only possibility','Recognition through different modalities must be assessed to separate naming from recognition.'),('A primary hand sensory deficit','Tactile recognition is preserved.')],
 'Recognition and sensation can dissociate', '**Agnosia is impaired recognition despite adequate primary sensory input.**', ['Test naming and nonverbal matching to refine the deficit.','Visual association networks are important.'], '**Exclude elementary vision and language explanations.**', ['Acuity, fields and comprehension need assessment.','Use modality comparisons rather than one naming question.'], [['Vision','Adequate input'],['Recognition','Impaired visually'],['Touch','Can be preserved']], [MT], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Acalculia, agraphia, finger agnosia and left-right confusion occur together. Which region is classically implicated?', 'Dominant inferior parietal/angular region',[
 ('Isolated nondominant cerebellar hemisphere','This does not explain the cortical cognitive cluster.'),('Neuromuscular junction','This does not mediate calculation and body schema.'),('A single peripheral nerve','It cannot explain the multidomain cortical syndrome.')],
 'A cluster can identify an association-cortex network', '**The classic Gerstmann tetrad implicates dominant parietal function.**', ['Real lesions may produce incomplete combinations.','Language and attention can confound bedside tests.'], '**Test each component carefully.**', ['Distinguish primary weakness from writing dysfunction.','Use imaging and the overall syndrome to refine the lesion.'], [['Calculation/writing','Dominant association function'],['Finger/right-left','Body-schema functions'],['Localization','Dominant inferior parietal network']], [MT])
q('A patient can write a sentence but cannot read it after a left occipital and splenial lesion. Which syndrome is most characteristic?', 'Alexia without agraphia',[
 ('Pure dysarthria','This does not explain selective reading failure.'),('A complete dominant motor-cortex lesion','That would not produce this classic disconnection pattern.'),('Peripheral neuropathy','This does not affect visual-language transfer in this way.')],
 'Disconnection can impair access to an intact language system', '**Left occipital injury with splenial involvement can disconnect visual input from dominant reading networks.**', ['A right homonymous field defect may coexist.','Writing can remain relatively preserved.'], '**Assess elementary vision and broader language.**', ['The syndrome is not diagnosed from slow reading alone.','Acute onset needs vascular assessment.'], [['Reading','Impaired'],['Writing','Relatively preserved'],['Classic lesion','Left occipital plus splenium']], [MT], 'clinical_scenario')
q('The right palate is weak and the uvula is pulled left during phonation, with hoarseness. Which peripheral cranial pathway is particularly implicated?', 'Right vagal motor pathway',[
 ('Left optic nerve','This does not supply the palate or larynx.'),('Right facial nerve as the sole motor supply','Palatal/laryngeal motor function is principally vagal.'),('Left hypoglossal nerve','This primarily affects tongue movement.')],
 'Palatal and laryngeal findings can travel together', '**Unilateral vagal weakness can reduce ipsilateral palatal elevation.**', ['The uvula is often drawn toward the stronger side.','Voice change and aspiration risk warrant direct assessment.'], '**Evaluate the full lower-cranial syndrome.**', ['Brainstem, skull-base and peripheral lesions are possible.','Gag reflex alone is an imperfect screening measure.'], [['Weak side','Reduced palatal elevation'],['Uvula','Often toward stronger side'],['Safety','Voice and swallowing assessment']], [EX], 'clinical_scenario')
q('What are the principal afferent and efferent limbs of the corneal blink reflex?', 'Trigeminal V1 afferent and facial VII efferent',[
 ('Optic II afferent and oculomotor III efferent','That describes the principal light-reflex pathways instead.'),('Vagus X afferent and hypoglossal XII efferent','These do not form the corneal blink arc.'),('Both limbs solely through the accessory nerve','The accessory nerve has another motor role.')],
 'A reflex tests an input and an output', '**Corneal sensation travels through V1; orbicularis closure is mediated by VII.**', ['Compare direct and consensual responses when appropriate.','Corneal disease and examination technique can confound findings.'], '**Protect the cornea during assessment.**', ['Use appropriate technique and avoid unnecessary trauma.','Interpret with facial power and facial sensation.'], [['Input','V1'],['Output','VII'],['Localization','Compare sensory and motor findings']], [CN])
q('A reduced Achilles reflex most directly assesses which predominant root contribution?', 'S1, with S2 contribution',[
 ('C5 as the principal root','This is relevant to upper-limb reflexes.'),('L3 as the sole root','This is not the principal Achilles contribution.'),('Cranial nerve XII','This supplies tongue motor function.')],
 'Reflexes map an arc rather than a whole disease', '**The ankle jerk samples the tibial-nerve/S1-dominant reflex arc.**', ['Peripheral neuropathy, root disease and other lesions can reduce it.','Age, relaxation and technique affect the response.'], '**Interpret asymmetry and accompanying signs.**', ['A single absent reflex is not a complete localization.','Use power and sensation to distinguish possibilities.'], [['Reflex','Achilles'],['Predominant root','S1'],['Arc','Peripheral and segmental components']], [EX])
q('A Hoffmann response is present on one side. Which interpretation is most appropriate?', 'It can support corticospinal dysfunction in context but is not diagnostic of cervical myelopathy by itself',[
 ('It proves a specific compressive lesion without imaging','The sign lacks that specificity.'),('It is a sensory nerve-conduction study','It is a bedside motor reflex sign.'),('Its absence excludes every cervical cord lesion','No single sign has perfect sensitivity.')],
 'Pathological reflex interpretation is probabilistic', '**Look for a coherent pattern of long-tract dysfunction.**', ['Gait, hand dexterity, tone and other reflexes matter.','Physiological briskness can complicate interpretation.'], '**Use symptoms and examination to decide investigation.**', ['Progressive clinical myelopathy merits assessment.','Do not diagnose or exclude disease from one maneuver.'], [['Single sign','Limited specificity'],['Pattern','Raises confidence'],['Imaging','Clinical question guides use']], [EX])
q('Voluntary hip extension seems weak but normal involuntary extension appears during resisted flexion of the opposite hip. What does this demonstrate?', 'A positive Hoover sign supporting functional weakness in an appropriate assessment',[
 ('Definite conscious fabrication','Functional signs do not establish deliberate intent.'),('A requirement for a prior psychiatric diagnosis','Diagnosis uses positive neurological signs.'),('Exclusion of all coexisting organic disease','Functional and structural disorders can coexist.')],
 'Use a positive inconsistency with a physiological basis', '**Hoover testing compares voluntary and automatic motor output.**', ['Pain and other confounders need consideration.','The finding is part of a full clinical assessment.'], '**Explain the result respectfully.**', ['A functional diagnosis can guide specific rehabilitation.','Do not equate symptom inconsistency with dishonesty.'], [['Voluntary output','Apparently reduced'],['Automatic output','Preserved in the maneuver'],['Meaning','Supports functional mechanism']], [FN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('How do nerve-conduction studies and needle EMG complement each other?', 'NCS assess peripheral nerve responses, while needle EMG assesses motor-unit and muscle electrical activity',[
 ('They are identical tests with no distinct information','They sample related but different physiology.'),('Both directly image spinal compression','Anatomical imaging is a different tool.'),('A normal routine NCS excludes every muscle or small-fiber disorder','It does not.')],
 'Choose tests for a localization question', '**Combined electrodiagnostic assessment can separate neuropathic, myopathic and transmission patterns.**', ['NCS include sensory and motor measurements.','Needle findings depend on the muscles and disease stage.'], '**State the suspected lesion in the referral.**', ['A focused examination guides sampling.','Anatomical MRI and physiology can be complementary.'], [['NCS','Nerve conduction responses'],['Needle EMG','Motor-unit/muscle activity'],['MRI','Structural anatomy']], [DI,EL])
q('Can a normal needle EMG obtained immediately after a new motor axon injury exclude denervation?', 'No; some denervation changes require time to develop and test timing affects sensitivity',[
 ('Yes, fibrillation appears instantly in every injury','Physiological changes evolve.'),('No, therefore timing never matters','Timing is precisely the issue.'),('A negative early study proves functional weakness','This conclusion is not warranted.')],
 'Electrical evidence has a biological time course', '**Wallerian and muscle membrane changes do not appear simultaneously with every injury.**', ['Distance, severity and the sampled muscles matter.','Early recruitment or conduction findings may still be informative.'], '**Repeat when it answers a useful clinical question.**', ['Do not delay urgent structural assessment for a later EMG.','Correlate the test with onset and localization.'], [['Early test','Can miss evolving denervation'],['Later test','May show clearer changes'],['Urgency','Structural emergencies still assessed promptly']], [EL])
q('When measuring LP opening pressure for diagnostic interpretation, what is the appropriate general method?', 'Measure before significant CSF removal in a properly positioned relaxed lateral-recumbent patient using the suitable protocol',[
 ('Use a sitting pressure interchangeably with a lateral reference range','Position affects interpretation.'),('Read the value only after removing a large volume','That no longer represents the initial opening pressure.'),('Treat one borderline number as sufficient regardless of the clinical context','Clinical and measurement factors matter.')],
 'Technique determines the meaning of a physiological number', '**Opening pressure requires standardized positioning and measurement.**', ['Straining and technical factors can distort it.','Assess LP safety and contraindications first.'], '**Interpret with symptoms, imaging and CSF composition.**', ['Do not use pressure alone to diagnose every headache disorder.','Document how the measurement was obtained.'], [['Position','Lateral recumbent'],['Timing','Before significant drainage'],['Interpretation','Clinical and technical context']], [LP])
q('Which principle best guides neurodiagnostic test selection?', 'Form a clinical localization and differential, then choose tests that can confirm, refute or change management',[
 ('Order every available test before examining the patient','This increases incidental findings and can obscure the question.'),('An abnormal test always explains every symptom','Incidental abnormalities are common.'),('A negative test excludes disease regardless of sensitivity or timing','Test limitations must be considered.')],
 'A test result needs a prior clinical question', '**Pretest probability, anatomical fit and assay limitations determine interpretation.**', ['Different tools assess anatomy, electrical function or biological markers.','Discordance should trigger reassessment rather than forced attribution.'], '**Connect results to a decision.**', ['Use additional testing when it resolves meaningful uncertainty.','Explain residual uncertainty clearly.'], [['Clinical assessment','Localization and probability'],['Test','Specific information'],['Decision','Meaningful consequence']], [DI,EX])

